494: \subsection{Dark matter halos}
495: The way in which dark matter is distributed on galactic length-scales has been a topic of much debate.  Over the past decade, the constant increase in computational power has allowed detailed $N$-body simulations of the clustering properties of cold dark matter particles to be carried out.  One of the first large-scale studies was carried out by \citet{NFW} in which they studied the equilibrium density profiles of dark matter halos in a hierarchial galaxy clustering scenario.  Their results suggested the existence of a ``universal dark matter density profile'' \citep{NFW_1997} which is independent of clustering scale, mass or size as well as the power spectrum of initial fluctuations.  Their parameterisation of this universal profile is
496: \begin{equation}
497: {\rho(r)_{NFW}\over \rho_{crit}}= {\delta_c \over {(r/r_s)(1+r/r_s)^2}}
498: \end{equation}
